From c1fec9467ca588df0164c8c624ad9729a13a3e58 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Adam Polkosnik <apolkosnik@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 10:32:28 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Disc images: 2048-byte-sector ISOs and MODE1/2048 cue tracks

A disc file that is not a .cue was always read as raw 2352-byte sectors,
so an .iso of 2048-byte sectors (how 3DO homebrew and most 3DO dumps
come) was refused: "not a raw image". Now:

- A lone file other than a .bin is read as 2048-byte sectors unless it
  starts with the CD sync pattern (a raw image named .iso still reads
  raw); .bin files are raw as before.
- A cue sheet's MODE1/2048 track reads its file as 2048-byte sectors
  (every track of a file must use the same sector size).
- Such a sector is returned raw, as a pressed disc has it: the sync
  pattern, the header (MSF address, mode 1), the 2048 bytes, the EDC and
  the P and Q ECC (ECMA-130).

Tested on the host with this file unchanged: all 56702 sectors of a raw
Wolfenstein 3D dump, rebuilt from their 2048-byte user data (as an .iso
and through a MODE1/2048 cue), are byte-identical to the dump, EDC and
ECC included; a raw image named .iso and a .bin read as before; a
MODE1/2048 data file plus an AUDIO file in one cue give the right TOC and
sectors on both sides of the boundary; mixed sector sizes in one file,
MODE2/2336, an ISO named .bin and a size that fits neither are refused
with a clear error.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
